### What the **callback URL** (redirect URI) is for

When you use SoundCloud’s OAuth 2 flow, the user is sent to SoundCloud to log in and grant permission to your app.  
After the user authorises, SoundCloud redirects the browser back to **your** site and appends an authorization code (or an error) to the URL you specify.  
That URL is called the **callback URL** (or **redirect URI**).

### How to choose a callback URL

| Situation | What to put in the callback URL | Example |
|-----------|----------------------------------|---------|
| **Production website** | A real page on your domain that can receive the `code` query‑parameter, process it (exchange it for an access token), and then finish the login flow. | `https://www.your‑site.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` |
| **Local development / testing** | Anything reachable by your browser while you’re developing. It can be a local server, a `localhost` address, or even a tool like **ngrok** that exposes a public URL to your local machine. | `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback` <br>or `https://abcd1234.ngrok.io/auth/soundcloud/callback` |
| **Static‑only site (no server)** | You can still use the **Implicit Grant** flow, which returns the access token directly in the fragment (`#access_token=…`). In that case the callback can be a simple HTML page that reads the fragment and stores the token (e.g., in `localStorage`). | `https://www.your‑site.com/soundcloud-auth.html` |

> **Important:** The exact URL you put in the **Redirect URI** field of your SoundCloud app settings **must match** the URL you use in the OAuth request (case‑sensitive, same protocol, same path, no trailing slash differences). If they don’t match, SoundCloud will reject the request with an *invalid redirect_uri* error.

### Minimal working example (Node/Express)

If you have a tiny backend, here’s a quick example that shows the whole flow:

```js
// server.js (Node + Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config();   // load CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET, REDIRECT_URI

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// 1️⃣ Step – send user to SoundCloud for auth
app.get('/auth/soundcloud', (req, res) => {
  const params = querystring.stringify({
    client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
    redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
    response_type: 'code',
    scope: '*',               // adjust scopes as needed
    state: 'someRandomCSRF',  // optional but recommended
  });
  res.redirect(`https://soundcloud.com/connect?${params}`);
});

// 2️⃣ Callback – SoundCloud redirects here with ?code=…
app.get('/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code } = req.query;
  if (!code) return res.send('No code returned – authentication failed.');

  // 3️⃣ Exchange the code for an access token
  const tokenRes = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: querystring.stringify({
      client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
      client_secret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET,
      redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code,
    }),
  });

  const tokenData = await tokenRes.json();
  // tokenData.access_token, tokenData.refresh_token, expires_in, etc.
  // Store the token (e.g., in a session or DB) and redirect to your UI.
  res.send(`✅ Auth successful!<br>Access token: ${tokenData.access_token}`);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));
```

**What you need to set in your `.env` (or directly in the SoundCloud app settings):**

```
CLIENT_ID=YOUR_CLIENT_ID
CLIENT_SECRET=YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET
REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback   # <-- your callback URL
```

### If you don’t have a backend (pure front‑end)

You can use the **Implicit Grant** flow, which returns the token in the URL fragment (`#access_token=…`).  
Create a simple HTML page that reads the fragment and stores the token:

```html
<!-- soundcloud-auth.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head><title>SoundCloud Auth</title></head>
<body>
  <script>
    // The fragment looks like: #access_token=XYZ&expires_in=3600&scope=*
    const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.hash.substring(1));
    const token = params.get('access_token');
    if (token) {
      // Store token where your app can read it (localStorage, sessionStorage, etc.)
      localStorage.setItem('sc_access_token', token);
      // Optionally redirect back to your main UI
      window.location.href = '/';
    } else {
      document.body.textContent = 'Authentication failed or was cancelled.';
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

When you start the flow, point the user to:

```
https://soundcloud.com/connect?
  client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID&
  redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.your-site.com%2Fsoundcloud-auth.html&
  response_type=token&
  scope=*
```

### Quick checklist before you finish the setup

1. **Add the exact URL** (including protocol `http://` or `https://`) to the **Redirect URI** field in your SoundCloud app dashboard.  
2. Make sure the URL is **publicly reachable** (or `localhost` if you’re testing locally).  
3. If you’re using a **development tunnel** (ngrok, Cloudflare‑Tunnel, etc.), copy the generated public URL into the dashboard – it can change each time you restart the tunnel, so update it whenever it changes.  
4. **Match the same URL** in the `redirect_uri` parameter of the OAuth request (the code snippet above does that automatically via `process.env.REDIRECT_URI`).  
5. **Save** the app settings on SoundCloud, then restart your local server (if you changed the URL).

### TL;DR – What to put in the callback field

- **Production:** `https://yourdomain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or any path you’ll handle).  
- **Local dev:** `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or the port you run your server on).  
- **Static‑only / front‑end only:** `https://yourdomain.com/soundcloud-auth.html` (a page that reads the `#access_token` fragment).

Once you have that URL registered, the OAuth flow will work and you can start playing tracks with the SoundCloud player or via the API (e.g., `soundCloudTrackUploader`, `soundCloudUserRecommender`, etc.).

