Below is a quick‑start guide you can drop into any web page to give form fields a **typing‑animation** effect as the user types.  
The solution is built with plain HTML, CSS, and a tiny bit of JavaScript, so it works in any modern browser without extra libraries.

---

## 1. Overview of the technique
1. **Wrap each `<input>` / `<textarea>` in a container** that also holds a pseudo‑element (`::after`) which will render the “cursor”.
2. **Listen for the `input` event** and copy the current value into a hidden `<span>` that mirrors the exact font, size, and spacing of the real field.
3. **Animate the hidden `<span>`** with CSS `@keyframes` so the characters appear one‑by‑one (typewriter effect).  
   The animation is restarted every time the user types, giving the illusion of a live typing animation.
4. **Show a blinking caret** using the pseudo‑element on the container.

---

## 2. HTML – Minimal markup

```html
<form class="typing-form">
  <div class="typing-field">
    <input type="text" id="name" name="name" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Your name">
    <span class="mirror" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="typing-field">
    <textarea id="msg" name="msg" rows="4" placeholder="Your message"></textarea>
    <span class="mirror" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>

  <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
```

* Each field lives inside a `.typing-field` wrapper.  
* The `<span class="mirror">` is invisible to screen‑readers (`aria-hidden`) and will hold the animated text.

---

## 3. CSS – Styling & animation

```css
/* -------------------------------------------------
   Basic layout
------------------------------------------------- */
.typing-form {
  max-width: 420px;
  margin: 2rem auto;
  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
}
.typing-field {
  position: relative;
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   Real input/textarea (transparent background)
------------------------------------------------- */
.typing-field input,
.typing-field textarea {
  width: 100%;
  padding: .6rem .8rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
  border: 2px solid #bbb;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background: transparent;          /* keep background transparent */
  color: #333;
  position: relative;
  z-index: 2;                        /* keep it above the mirror */
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   Mirror element – hidden but used for animation
------------------------------------------------- */
.typing-field .mirror {
  position: absolute;
  top: .6rem; left: .8rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
  white-space: pre;                 /* preserve spaces */
  overflow: hidden;
  pointer-events: none;
  color: transparent;               /* we only need the width for animation */
  z-index: 1;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   Cursor (blinking caret) – attached to the wrapper
------------------------------------------------- */
.typing-field::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: .6rem; left: .8rem;
  width: 2px;
  height: 1.2rem;
  background: #333;
  animation: blink .8s steps(1) infinite;
  z-index: 3;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   Typewriter animation
------------------------------------------------- */
@keyframes typewriter {
  from { width: 0; }
  to   { width: var(--text-width); }
}
@keyframes blink {
  0%, 50% { opacity: 1; }
  51%, 100% { opacity: 0; }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   When the user types, we set a CSS variable with the
   exact width of the text and trigger the animation.
------------------------------------------------- */
.typing-field.typing .mirror {
  animation: typewriter .4s steps(var(--char-count)) forwards;
}
```

**Explanation of the CSS tricks**

| Piece | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| `.mirror` | Holds the same text as the real field, but its color is transparent. Its width is animated, revealing characters one‑by‑one. |
| `--text-width` | A custom property we set from JavaScript to the exact pixel width of the current text. |
| `--char-count` | Number of characters; used as the `steps()` count so the animation stops exactly at each character. |
| `::after` | The blinking caret that follows the animated text. Its `left` position is the same as the mirror’s left edge, so it appears right after the last revealed character. |
| `.typing` class | Added by JS while the user is typing; it triggers the animation. |

